,post_data : {"post_id":22265700,"post_type":"view_posts_text","topic_id":0,"poster_id":226233,"post_rendered":"June 6th, 2021","post_canonical":"June 6th, 2021","username":"steppewolf","post_number":0,"post_format":"bbcode","category_name":"2021 Junior Macedonian Mathematical Olympiad","category_id":2330856,"poster_avatar":"\/\/avatar.artofproblemsolving.com\/avatar_226233.png?t=1456590674"}});
AoPS.Community.categories[7].items.push({item_id : 22265056,item_text : "Problem 1",item_forum_id : 0,item_type : "post",item_main_color : "",item_secondary_color : ""
,post_data : {"post_id":22265056,"post_type":"forum","topic_id":2585122,"poster_id":226233,"post_rendered":"At this year&#039;s Olympiad, some of the students are friends (friendship is symmetric), however there are also students which are not friends. No matter how the students are partitioned in two contest halls, there are always two friends in different halls. Let <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/0\/1\/9\/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" > be a fixed student. Show that there exist students <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/f\/f\/5\/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" > and <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/c\/3\/3\/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" > such that there are exactly two friendships in the group <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/e\/3\/c\/e3c853d151d221bc299ebe9de6a9b13d48ba2106.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{ A,B,C \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" >.<\/span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Authored by Mirko Petrushevski<\/i>","post_canonical":"At this year's Olympiad, some of the students are friends (friendship is symmetric), however there are also students which are not friends. No matter how the students are partitioned in two contest halls, there are always two friends in different halls. Let $A$ be a fixed student. Show that there exist students $B$ and $C$ such that there are exactly two friendships in the group $\\{ A,B,C \\}$.\n\n[i]Authored by Mirko Petrushevski[\/i]","username":"steppewolf","post_number":1,"post_format":"bbcode","category_name":"High School Olympiads","category_id":6,"poster_avatar":"\/\/avatar.artofproblemsolving.com\/avatar_226233.png?t=1456590674"}});
AoPS.Community.categories[7].items.push({item_id : 22264889,item_text : "Problem 2",item_forum_id : 0,item_type : "post",item_main_color : "",item_secondary_color : ""
,post_data : {"post_id":22264889,"post_type":"forum","topic_id":2585113,"poster_id":226233,"post_rendered":"Let <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/f\/9\/e\/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\"  width=\"57\" height=\"13\" > be a tangential quadrilateral with inscribed circle <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/8\/b\/8\/8b8cc5e7379f500e9e554e95ede04d658542e3e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k(O,r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" > which is tangent to the sides <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/6\/c\/5\/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > and <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/b\/c\/5\/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"13\" > at <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/d\/f\/b\/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/8\/5\/9\/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" >,<\/span> respectively. Show that the circle with diameter <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/2\/0\/f\/20f8f2f1a3ddc6b42fae25a0637e85c747875180.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > passes through the intersection point of <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/e\/f\/0\/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/4\/6\/5\/46569d6dd3fa86f698827e8de15b0480e1733d11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" >.<\/span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Ilija Jovchevski<\/i>","post_canonical":"Let $ABCD$ be a tangential quadrilateral with inscribed circle $k(O,r)$ which is tangent to the sides $BC$ and $AD$ at $K$ and $L$, respectively. Show that the circle with diameter $OC$ passes through the intersection point of $KL$ and $OD$.\n\n[i]Proposed by Ilija Jovchevski[\/i]","username":"steppewolf","post_number":1,"post_format":"bbcode","category_name":"High School Olympiads","category_id":6,"poster_avatar":"\/\/avatar.artofproblemsolving.com\/avatar_226233.png?t=1456590674"}});
AoPS.Community.categories[7].items.push({item_id : 22264821,item_text : "Problem 3",item_forum_id : 0,item_type : "post",item_main_color : "",item_secondary_color : ""
,post_data : {"post_id":22264821,"post_type":"forum","topic_id":2585107,"poster_id":226233,"post_rendered":"Find all positive integers <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/1\/7\/4\/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > and prime numbers <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/3\/6\/f\/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" > such that <img src=\"\/\/latex.artofproblemsolving.com\/8\/1\/d\/81d15243794c87865221233dbe1af896f6668ff0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"$$17^n \\cdot 2^{n^2} - p =(2^{n^2+3}+2^{n^2}-1) \\cdot n^2.$$\"  width=\"288\" height=\"23\" ><br>\n<i>Authored by Nikola Velov<\/i>","post_canonical":"Find all positive integers $n$ and prime numbers $p$ such that $$17^n \\cdot 2^{n^2} - p =(2^{n^2+3}+2^{n^2}-1) \\cdot n^2.$$\n\n[i]Authored by Nikola Velov[\/i]","username":"steppewolf","post_number":1,"post_format":"bbcode","category_name":"High School Olympiads","category_id":6,"poster_avatar":"\/\/avatar.artofproblemsolving.com\/avatar_226233.png?t=1456590674"}});
